  "worked_solution": "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=2Z,\\,y=3Y,\\,z=5X\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n\u27e8r,Z\u27e9=\u27e8\\operatorname{cosh}\u2061\u03d5,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}\u2061\u03d5\u27e9,\\,\u03d5\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,\u03d5) & =\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82Z,3Y,5X\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82Z,\\,3r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82\\operatorname{sinh}\u2061\u03d5,\\,3(\\operatorname{cosh}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,5(\\operatorname{cosh}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82\\operatorname{sinh}\u2061\u03d5,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $\u03d5\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{cosh}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta .$",
